 "domain": "Number and Operations in Base Ten",
 "standard_code": "3.NBT.A.1",
 "standard_url": "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/3/NBT/A/1/",
 "standard_text": "Use place value understanding to round whole numbers to the nearest 10 or 100.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 3 rounding worksheets: round to the nearest 10 and 100 with number lines, the 5 rule, and estimating sums and differences. 100 practice questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Look one place to the right. 5 or more: round up. 4 or less: round down.",
 "key_sub": "Rounding finds the closest ten or hundred. A number ending in 5 is exactly halfway, and it always rounds up.",
 "body_html": "<h2>What rounding means</h2><p>Rounding finds the <b>closest</b> ten or hundred. It makes numbers easier to add and subtract in your head, and it helps you check whether an answer makes sense.</p><figure class='sn-fig'><svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 95' width='400' height='95' role='img' aria-label='number line from 40 to 50'><line x1='30' y1='50' x2='370' y2='50' stroke='#333' stroke-width='2'/><line x1='30.0' y1='40' x2='30.0' y2='60' stroke='#333' stroke-width='2'/><text x='30.0' y='80' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#333' text-anchor='middle'>40</text><line x1='64.0' y1='44' x2='64.0' y2='56' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='98.0' y1='44' x2='98.0' y2='56' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='132.0' y1='44' x2='132.0' y2='56' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='166.0' y1='44' x2='166.0' y2='56' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='200.0' y1='40' x2='200.0' y2='60' stroke='#333' stroke-width='2'/><text x='200.0' y='80' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#333' text-anchor='middle'>45</text><line x1='234.0' y1='44' x2='234.0' y2='56' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='268.0' y1='44' x2='268.0' y2='56' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='302.0' y1='44' x2='302.0' y2='56' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='336.0' y1='44' x2='336.0' y2='56' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1'/><line x1='370.0' y1='40' x2='370.0' y2='60' stroke='#333' stroke-width='2'/><text x='370.0' y='80' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#333' text-anchor='middle'>50</text><circle cx='268.0' cy='50' r='8' fill='#c0392b'/><text x='268.0' y='32' font-size='16' font-weight='800' fill='#c0392b' text-anchor='middle'>47</text></svg><figcaption>47 is closer to 50 than to 40, so 47 rounds to 50.</figcaption></figure><h2>The rounding rule</h2><ol class='rk-step'><li>Find the digit in the place you are rounding to.</li><li>Look at the digit <b>one place to the right</b>.</li><li><b>5 or more</b>: round up. <b>4 or less</b>: round down.</li><li>Change every digit after the rounding place to 0.</li></ol><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Number</th><th>Nearest ten</th><th>Nearest hundred</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>63</td><td>60</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>245</td><td>250</td><td>200</td></tr><tr><td>378</td><td>380</td><td>400</td></tr><tr><td>850</td><td>850</td><td>900</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Round 365 to the nearest hundred.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>The hundreds digit is 3. Look right: the tens digit is 6 (5 or more), so round up.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 400</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Estimate</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>About how much is 48 + 33?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>48 rounds to 50 and 33 rounds to 30. 50 + 30 = 80.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: about 80</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Looking at the wrong digit</td><td>Always look one place to the right of the rounding place</td></tr><tr><td>Rounding 45 down to 40</td><td>5 rounds up: 45 → 50</td></tr><tr><td>Writing 368 → 370 for nearest hundred</td><td>Nearest hundred ends in 00: 368 → 400</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you teach rounding to the nearest ten?",
   "a": "Put the number on a number line between two tens and ask which ten it is closer to. Once that makes sense, teach the shortcut: look at the ones digit, 5 or more rounds up."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why does 5 round up?",
   "a": "A number ending in 5 is exactly halfway between two tens. Mathematicians agreed to round it up so everyone gets the same answer."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why do 3rd graders learn rounding?",
   "a": "Rounding is used to estimate. Estimating an answer first is the quickest way to catch a mistake in addition, subtraction and later in multiplication."
  }
 ],
